3. Half duplex, synchronous serial communication protocol 

4. Specifically designed for onboard (intra board) IC communication
	
	Note : less than 1 foot (30cm) communication with a connector

5. I2C is multimaster and multi slave communication protocol 
	
6. I2C bus master can use 100Kbps (standard), 400 Kbps (fast mode), 1 Mbps (fast mode plus) or 3.4 Mbps (High speed) for communication 

7. Each device connected to the I2C bus has a unique address, known as slave address / device address 
   The slave address can be 7 bits or 10 bits 

   Limitation 
   ----------

	* If slaves with same address is connected it will fail 
	* We cannot connect same type of slaves on the same i2c bus (diff bus we can)

8. ACK based protocol 

	Each byte of data (including the address byte) is followed by 1 ack bit from the receiver 
	
	Size of ack bit = 1

9. Data is transferred from MSB first 

10. I2C has an arbitration policy 


	Priorities assigned on master 
	-----------------------------

	M1 = 0000 1010
	M2 = 0000 0010

	Lets compare bit-by-bit (MSB to LSB)

	| Bit position | M1 | M2 | SDA result | Who wins    |
	| ------------ | -- | -- | ---------- | ----------- |
	|       1      | 0  | 0  | 0          | both stay   |
	|       2      | 0  | 0  | 0          | both stay   |
	|       3      | 0  | 0  | 0          | both stay   |
	|       4      | 0  | 0  | 0          | both stay   |
	|       5      | 1  | 0  | 0          | **M2 wins** |


	In I2C, the master that transmits the first dominant 0 while the others transmits 1 become the winner.


































 	
		


















































